+//
+// NOT SHIPPED and reachable from no config key. `MbParams::fillHermite` is set
+// by the adapter from HYBMESH_MB_FILL_HERMITE, and this block exists so that the
+// spike's measurement table can be produced against the same binary that builds
+// the shipped meshes. The whole of it: docs/research/hermite-wall-normal-fill/.
+//
+// THE PRESCRIPTION is Thompson, Warsi & Mastin Ch. VIII §1.B — replace the
+// linear Lagrange blend in one direction with a cubic Hermite one whose boundary
+// derivative is set ALONG THE WALL NORMAL with a magnitude fixed by the declared
+// off-wall spacing, which makes the grid orthogonal at the boundary with that
+// spacing. What it targets here is the offset ring's tilted chord
+// (docs/research/arc-length-correspondence-on-an-offset-ring.md): the interior
+// radial between two nodes at equal arc fraction is longer than the ring is
+// thick, so the first cell is stretched by that ratio.
+//
+// WRITTEN AS AN ADDITIVE CORRECTION, not as a second fill, and that shape does
+// three things the alternative does not:
+//   * with the two tangents set to the LINEAR-EQUIVALENT `T = E - W`, the cubic
+//     Hermite blend collapses algebraically to `W + u(E - W)` — `H10 + H11 =
+//     2u³ - 3u² + u` cancels `H00`/`H01`'s cubic part exactly — so the whole
+//     change is carried by how far each end's tangent DEPARTS from that;
+//   * it VANISHES on all four sides, so the boundary is still the edge's own
+//     discretisation and nothing about the welding changes;
+//   * the two directions' corrections simply ADD. What superposition drops is
+//     the TWIST — the cross derivative at a corner where both directions carry a
+//     wall — and that is a real approximation of this prototype, stated rather
+//     than hidden.
+//
+// THERE ARE TWO VARIANTS, and the second exists because the first has a measured
+// accuracy limit that is NOT the prescription's fault:
+//   1 = the LITERAL derivative condition. `T = req/gap * n̂`, so the first
+//       interior node sits at `wall + req * n̂` to first order in `gap`. Its
+//       residual is `gap(2 - gap)` times the linear fill's own first-cell error
+//       vector, which is small only where `gap` is; and it is calibrated against
+//       the pure `u`-term `gap * chord`, which is NOT what the transfinite fill
+//       puts on the first interior line when the two facing perpendicular edges
+//       carry different laws. Measured on the shipped H-grid, whose `h00` asks
+//       for 1.504e-01 at one end and 2.000e-01 at the other: 0.15% -> 4.40%.
+//   2 = the same cubic correction CALIBRATED AGAINST THE FILL'S OWN FIRST
+//       INTERIOR LINE. `δ = (req * n̂ - actual first step) / H(gap)`, forced to
+//       zero at the side's two END stations, where the perpendicular edge's own
+//       discretisation is the answer and the boolean sum's compatibility
+//       condition says the corner derivative is shared. This is the best-faith
+//       version: it hits the declared height in the declared direction to the
+//       accuracy of one cubic term, so a NO measured with it is a statement
+//       about the idea rather than about the arithmetic.
+double mbH10(double t) { return t * (1.0 - t) * (1.0 - t); }
+double mbH11(double t) { return t * t * (t - 1.0); }
+
+// THE WALL'S UNIT TANGENT AT EACH STATION, and the two END stations are the ones
+// that had to be got right. A one-sided `wall[1] - wall[0]` is the chord of the
+// first interval, which on a curved wall is tilted from the tangent by half the
+// turn across it — 1.9 deg on the shipped O-grid's body arc — and that error does
+// not stay at the corner, because the correction subtracts a blend of the two END
+// deltas from every station. Measured with the one-sided version, the O-grid's
+// unsmoothed wall first-cell error went 0.000036 -> 0.024536: the prototype's own
+// arithmetic, not the prescription. The ends therefore EXTRAPOLATE the two
+// adjacent facet directions (`1.5*d01 - 0.5*d12`), which is exact on a circle to
+// the order the interior's central difference is.
+std::vector<Point2D> mbWallTangents(const std::vector<Point2D>& wall) {
+    const size_t n = wall.size();
+    std::vector<Point2D> tang(n, Point2D{0.0, 0.0});
+    if (n < 2) return tang;
+    std::vector<Point2D> dir(n - 1, Point2D{0.0, 0.0});
+    for (size_t k = 0; k + 1 < n; ++k) dir[k] = (wall[k + 1] - wall[k]).normalized();
+    for (size_t k = 0; k < n; ++k) {
+        if (k > 0 && k + 1 < n)   tang[k] = (wall[k + 1] - wall[k - 1]).normalized();
+        else if (n < 3)           tang[k] = dir[0];
+        else if (k == 0)          tang[k] = (dir[0] * 1.5 - dir[1] * 0.5).normalized();
+        else                      tang[k] = (dir[n - 2] * 1.5 - dir[n - 3] * 0.5)
+                                                .normalized();
+    }
+    return tang;
